{
154: {skill_tested: "Identify a stated contrast in characters' work methods.", question: "According to the third paragraph, how do Julian's and Mara's approaches differ?", option1: "Julian reads every paper, while Mara tests projector equipment.", option2: "Julian wants to keep everything, while Mara wants to discard everything.", option3: "Julian works only upstairs, while Mara refuses to enter the lobby.", option4: "Julian documents equipment for sale, while Mara examines objects and papers for personal meaning.", correctAnswer: "Julian documents equipment for sale, while Mara examines objects and papers for personal meaning.", explanation: "Step 1: Julian tests, records, and photographs equipment.<br><br>Step 2: Mara opens drawers and attends to found objects and unsent notes.<br><br>Answer: Julian documents equipment for sale, while Mara examines objects and papers for personal meaning.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Character and Relationship Analysis", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
155: {skill_tested: "Determine the contextual meaning of a descriptive term.", question: "As used in the final paragraph, the word 'restrained' most nearly describes an action that is:", option1: "deliberately kept from becoming overly symbolic or emotional.", option2: "physically prevented by broken lights.", option3: "performed with great speed.", option4: "intended to preserve the building unchanged.", correctAnswer: "deliberately kept from becoming overly symbolic or emotional.", explanation: "Step 1: Mara feels an urge to turn the broken lights into a symbol but resists it.<br><br>Step 2: Her response remains controlled and limited to standing beside Julian, so 'restrained' describes deliberate emotional moderation.<br><br>Answer: deliberately kept from becoming overly symbolic or emotional.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Vocabulary in Context", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
156: {skill_tested: "Identify a central theme about memory and relationships.", question: "Which statement best expresses a central theme of the passage?", option1: "Accurate records make personal memories unnecessary.", option2: "Different, incomplete ways of remembering can correct and deepen one another.", option3: "Family conflict ends only when inherited property is preserved.", option4: "Objects possess meaning only when their histories can be proven exactly.", correctAnswer: "Different, incomplete ways of remembering can correct and deepen one another.", explanation: "Step 1: Mara values stories and objects but invents an inaccurate history for the film can.<br><br>Step 2: Julian values documentation but initially dismisses items whose significance is not operational.<br><br>Answer: Different, incomplete ways of remembering can correct and deepen one another.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
157: {skill_tested: "Infer how childhood roles shaped a later misunderstanding.", question: "The second paragraph suggests that Mara and Julian's adult conflict is partly rooted in:", option1: "their father having forbidden them to work inside the cinema.", option2: "both wanting to operate the projectors as teenagers.", option3: "each having underestimated the complexity of the other's earlier responsibilities.", option4: "Mara having kept formal records of every discounted ticket.", correctAnswer: "each having underestimated the complexity of the other's earlier responsibilities.", explanation: "Step 1: Mara worked at the ticket window while Julian stayed near the projectors.<br><br>Step 2: Each privately judged the other's assignment easier.<br><br>Step 3: Their adult tendency to treat the other's method as deficient continues this long-standing failure to see the work behind it.<br><br>Answer: each having underestimated the complexity of the other's earlier responsibilities.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Character and Relationship Analysis", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
158: {skill_tested: "Determine how documentary evidence revises a character judgment.", question: "The notebooks most directly challenge Mara's belief that Julian:", option1: "cannot operate or maintain the projectors.", option2: "remembers the cinema's opening night accurately.", option3: "has already donated every record to the film club.", option4: "is indifferent to the cinema and interested only in disposing of equipment.", correctAnswer: "is indifferent to the cinema and interested only in disposing of equipment.", explanation: "Step 1: Mara interprets Julian's speed and photographs as refusal to look or care.<br><br>Step 2: The notebooks reveal years of maintenance, and his photographs are intended for a community archive.<br><br>Step 3: His care has taken documentary and technical forms that Mara did not recognize.<br><br>Answer: is indifferent to the cinema and interested only in disposing of equipment.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
159: {skill_tested: "Analyze how one object exposes weaknesses in both perspectives.", question: "The empty film can primarily serves to show that:", option1: "Mara's memory can invent significance while Julian's initial sorting can dismiss an object before examining it.", option2: "every discarded cinema object contains an accurate family history.", option3: "Julian remembers opening night more clearly than Mara does.", option4: "technical records are less dependable than faded labels.", correctAnswer: "Mara's memory can invent significance while Julian's initial sorting can dismiss an object before examining it.", explanation: "Step 1: Mara confidently assigns the can an opening-night history that its scratched date disproves.<br><br>Step 2: Julian initially discards it without noticing that date or the argument it will come to represent.<br><br>Step 3: The object exposes both sentimental overinterpretation and overly rapid classification.<br><br>Answer: Mara's memory can invent significance while Julian's initial sorting can dismiss an object before examining it.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Function and Structure", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
160: {skill_tested: "Trace how revised categories reflect relationship change.", question: "Replacing three sorting labels with four most strongly indicates that the siblings:", option1: "have agreed that every item belongs in the family collection.", option2: "are creating categories capable of representing both documentary and personal forms of value.", option3: "no longer plan to sell or recycle any cinema property.", option4: "have decided the film club should determine all classifications.", correctAnswer: "are creating categories capable of representing both documentary and personal forms of value.", explanation: "Step 1: The original labels reduce decisions to possession, sale, or disposal.<br><br>Step 2: CLUB ARCHIVE recognizes public documentary value, while FAMILY recognizes relational value that may not depend on an accurate origin story.<br><br>Step 3: The new system is a practical compromise shaped by both siblings' revised understanding.<br><br>Answer: are creating categories capable of representing both documentary and personal forms of value.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Sequence and Relationships", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
161: {skill_tested: "Synthesize the distinction between cooperation and complete agreement.", question: "Why does the passage emphasize that Mara's and Julian's methods have not merged by evening?", option1: "To show that their cooperation depends on abandoning the sorting task", option2: "To prove that the four new categories have failed", option3: "To clarify that their relationship improves through mutual correction without requiring identical habits", option4: "To suggest that neither sibling has learned anything about the other", correctAnswer: "To clarify that their relationship improves through mutual correction without requiring identical habits", explanation: "Step 1: Julian continues photographing, and Mara continues reading each scrap.<br><br>Step 2: He now asks about unknown histories, while she no longer treats obscurity as sufficient value.<br><br>Step 3: Their distinct methods remain, but each becomes answerable to evidence supplied by the other.<br><br>Answer: To clarify that their relationship improves through mutual correction without requiring identical habits", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Character and Relationship Analysis", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
162: {skill_tested: "Interpret a qualified ending through earlier character development.", question: "The final phrase 'made answerable to each other' most nearly suggests that the siblings' memories now:", option1: "must be identical before either can preserve an object.", option2: "have been replaced entirely by their father's notebooks.", option3: "prevent them from accepting the building's sale.", option4: "are tested and supplemented by the other's different evidence and attention.", correctAnswer: "are tested and supplemented by the other's different evidence and attention.", explanation: "Step 1: Mara's story about the can is corrected by a date, while Julian's apparent indifference is corrected by notebooks and archival purpose.<br><br>Step 2: Their revised sorting system incorporates personal and documentary value.<br><br>Step 3: Being answerable means neither way of remembering operates as unquestioned truth; each must respond to what the other reveals.<br><br>Answer: are tested and supplemented by the other's different evidence and attention.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"}
}
